  "worked_solution": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ only.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
